     "a": 0
    },
    {
     "type": "tip",
     "tag": "British or American?",
     "title": "Spanner or wrench?",
     "paras": [
      "British English says <b>spanner</b>, American English says <b>wrench</b>. On board you'll hear both. They're the same tool.",
      "<b>Safety glasses</b> are also called <i>goggles</i> when they close around the eyes."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "tip",
     "tag": "Pronunciation",
     "title": "The -ing ending",
     "paras": [
      "<i>-ing</i> is one sound: /ɪŋ/. The tongue goes back, like the end of <i>sing</i>. No hard <i>g</i> after it: not <i>lashin-g</i>.",
      "In fast talk it often sounds like <i>-in</i>: <i>chippin', lashin'</i>. You'll hear that from Rolando."
     ],
     "list": [
